3. Cloud-provider service ranges — plugin/assets/data/
------------------------------------------------------------------

Files:
  assets/data/gr-dch.txt    (text CIDR list, one per line, with a
                             provenance header)
  assets/data/gr-dch.php    (packed start/end range arrays the
                             runtime classifier loads once per
                             request; generated, do not edit)

Sources:
  - AWS official public service ranges
    https://ip-ranges.amazonaws.com/ip-ranges.json
  - Google official public service ranges
    https://www.gstatic.com/ipranges/goog.json
  - Microsoft Azure official Service Tags (download page
    https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=56519;
    the dated JSON link is discovered from the page)
  All three are published by the providers as public operational
  data for exactly this purpose and are fetched without any account
  or credentials.
  - IP2Proxy LITE (PX database, DCH rows only — data center/web
    hosting/colocation segments)
    https://lite.ip2location.com/

License:  The official cloud segment tables are public operational
          data of their respective providers.
          IP2Proxy LITE database is licensed under
          CC BY-SA 4.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
          Attribution is met by this notice and the plugin readme;
          terms also ask that users are told they can register at
          lite.ip2location.com themselves to fetch updated copies
          (stated on the plugin's IP Intelligence page).

The build tool tools/pack-dch.php merges and sorts the per-family
ranges (adjacent and overlapping ranges fold; IPv4 stays numeric,
IPv6 as 32-char lowercase hex). The admin refresh button re-fetches
the three official tables on the owner's explicit click and writes
the merged copy to the uploads directory; it never modifies the
plugin directory, and a failed refresh keeps the current data.

Data date: 2026-09-15 (1,029 merged IPv4 ranges, 2,193 merged IPv6
ranges in the bundled copy). Later refreshes record their own build
date inside the packed file; this notice states the date of the copy
bundled with the release.

